# ORCLRIDE: what conveyors carry, and what a spinning gun fires (M16.4).
#
# ORCLDEV pinned these three elements' animation and their cycle-gate
# scheduling, but deliberately left their ACTING paths untested: its conveyor
# rings were empty and its gun carried p2 0, because a title board that moves
# things cannot be replayed (the `boot 240` span is not a cycle-accurate model of
# real ZZT booting, so the phase search absorbs boot-count error for animation
# and never for accumulated state). This scenario closes that gap the way
# ORCLMECH and ORCLBLNK do: the devices sit on a board reached over a board edge,
# so everything they do happens inside the fully modelled play span. `phase` is
# still declared, because all three still draw straight off CurrentTick.
#
# ElementConveyorTick walks the eight diagonal neighbours in ring order and
# shuffles every Pushable tile round by one, but only across runs the scan has
# decided can move: an empty square re-arms the run, and anything not pushable
# stops it. Two rings, one of each handedness, separate those cases:
#
#   Clockwise at 10,8 — a free ring carrying a boulder and a gem, which simply
#     revolve, one step per tick, in opposite corners of the same ring
#   Counter at 20,8 — the same idea with a SOLID square sitting in the ring at
#     19,8, so the scan's run is broken there and the cargo cannot come round
#
# ElementSpinningGunTick's firing half is two nested random draws, and both are
# forced here rather than avoided: p2 9 makes `Random(9) < p2 mod 128` true for
# every possible draw, and p1 8 makes `Random(9) <= p1` true for every possible
# draw. So the gun always fires and always fires the AIMED shot, and the compared
# path stays free of RNG-dependent outcomes even though the draws still happen.
# What is left is the aim itself, which has two branches, one per gun:
#
#   40,13 — the player's row: |dx| is 39, too far for the vertical branch, so it
#     falls through to the horizontal one and fires west down the row, into a
#     solid backstop at 30,13
#   2,20  — the player's column, near enough (|dx| is 1) for the vertical branch,
#     so it fires north up its own column into a backstop at 2,15
#
# Both guns fire on every one of their ticks, so each checkpoint catches a
# steady stream of bullets in flight; their spacing is the thing being compared,
# and it comes out of the gun's cycle against the bullets' own.
#
# The checkpoints are spaced by deliberately awkward intervals. The clockwise
# ring's revolution is 24 frames and the bullet columns repeat every 4, so evenly
# spaced captures all land on the same instant of both and prove far less than
# they appear to.
world ORCLRIDE
seed 0
phase
boot 240
capture title
play
# Ride Hub: unpause and cross the east border opening at 60,13.
move right
move right
move right
move right
move right
capture arrive
settle 14
capture riding-a
settle 14
capture riding-b
settle 22
capture riding-c
settle 26
capture riding-d
settle 34
capture riding-e
settle 42
capture riding-f
